        "rationale": "When $-3y$ and $+2$ are collected, the right-hand side is $3y + 2$."
      },
      {
        "key": "C",
        "content": "$x = \\dfrac{3y + 2}{y + 1}$",
        "rationale": "$xy - x = x(y - 1)$, not $x(y + 1)$."
      },
      {
        "key": "D",
        "content": "$x = \\dfrac{y + 2}{y - 3}$",
        "rationale": "This is the original formula with $x$ replaced by $y$; it does not make $x$ the subject."
      }
    ],
    "explanation": "Multiply by $(x - 3)$: $y(x - 3) = x + 2$, so $xy - 3y = x + 2$. Collect the $x$ terms: $xy - x = 3y + 2$. Factorise: $x(y - 1) = 3y + 2$, so $x = \\frac{3y + 2}{y - 1}$.",
    "hints": [
      "Multiply both sides by the denominator first.",
      "Then collect every term containing $x$ on one side and factorise."
    ],
