            "correct": true,
            "rationale": "$m$ is a common factor of both terms."
          },
          {
            "key": "B",
            "content": "$E = m(gh + v^2)$",
            "rationale": "The $\\frac{1}{2}$ belongs to the second term."
          },
          {
            "key": "C",
            "content": "$E = mgh\\left(1 + \\frac{1}{2}v^2\\right)$",
            "rationale": "$gh$ is not a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}mv^2$."
          },
          {
            "key": "D",
            "content": "$E = m^2\\left(gh + \\frac{1}{2}v^2\\right)$",
            "rationale": "Only one $m$ is taken out."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "(b)",
        "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
        "marks": 2,
        "stem": "Make $m$ the subject of the formula.",
        "options": [
          {
            "key": "A",
            "content": "$m = \\dfrac{2E}{2gh + v^2}$",
            "correct": true,
            "rationale": "$m = \\frac{E}{gh + \\frac{1}{2}v^2}$; multiply top and bottom by 2."
          },
          {
            "key": "B",
            "content": "$m = \\dfrac{E}{gh} - \\frac{1}{2}v^2$",
            "rationale": "The subject appears in both terms, so it must be factorised out, not moved term by term."
          },
          {
            "key": "C",
            "content": "$m = \\dfrac{2E}{gh + v^2}$",
            "rationale": "Multiplying the denominator by 2 gives $2gh + v^2$."
          },
          {
            "key": "D",
            "content": "$m = \\dfrac{E - gh}{\\frac{1}{2}v^2}$",
            "rationale": "$gh$ is multiplied by $m$, so it cannot be subtracted from $E$ on its own."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
